# Kid Niki sound board (Irem M62) -- netlist converted from MAME
#
# Couriersud's netlist of the Irem M62 sound board, made from a KiCad redraw of the Kid
# Niki schematic (the file keeps the EESCHEMA header), so the designators are the redraw's:
# XU1 (LM2902), XU2 (LM358), XU3 (MC14584B), written U1-U3 here; R65_1 and R48_2 are the
# redraw's names for parts the schematic numbers again. The AY-3-8910s and MSM5205s are
# MAME devices; what they drive arrives as ports. Three of MAME's judgements about the
# schematic are kept as MAME has them, all switches at the top of its file: FIX_SCHEMATIC_
# ERRORS (XU1 section B given the feedback the schematic omits, and D4, D5 and Q4 wired as
# the Kung-Fu Master schematic's manual corrections have them); D4_AND_D5_CONNECTED, which
# MAME marks 'needs verification'; and J4 set to take channel C to the sound IC.
#
# Source: MAME mame0289 (commit f34f02505e32), src/mame/irem/nl_kidniki.cpp,
# NETLIST_START(kidniki). Licence BSD-3-Clause by the file's own header (line 1),
# copyright-holders Andrew Gardner, Couriersud (line 2).
# https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mamedev/mame/mame0289/src/mame/irem/nl_kidniki.cpp
# Converted by tools/mame_netlist.py; checked by tools/build_board_nets.py.
#
# What the conversion means. MAME's netlists are written to be simulated, so this is a
# second reading of the sheet by someone else, not a read of it: designators and pin
# numbers are MAME's unless the notes say the sheet was read to correct them. Names on
# `label` lines are MAME's, which mostly follow the sheet's with Q for an overbar; `net`
# is used only for the supply rails. A `port` is a net that leaves what this file
# describes -- a signal from the CPU or the other board, a cabinet control, or a source
# MAME simulates with a stand-in rather than the circuit.
#
#   net NAME: PIN ...     a supply rail
#   label NAME: PIN ...   a net MAME names
#   wire PIN PIN ...      an unnamed connection
#   port NAME: PIN ...    a net that leaves this file (see above)
#   nc PIN ...            a pin with nothing attached
#   parallel PIN ...      outputs the board sums on one node by design
#   value REF VALUE       a passive's value, in the unit MAME's own macro uses
#
# PIN is DESIGNATOR.NUMBER. Diode pin 1 is the cathode; transistors keep B, C and E;
# MAME does not record which way round an electrolytic goes, so C pins are MAME's 1/2.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Undone: what MAME does to simulate the board that the board does not do
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# line 9: #define USE_FRONTIERS 1 -> 0 (solver partitioning only)
# line 15: #define USE_FIXED_STV 0 -- kept as MAME has it (not a known kind of hack)
# line 42: #define FIX_SCHEMATIC_ERRORS 1 -- kept as MAME has it (not a known kind of hack)
#
# Corrected from sheet 4/4 of the M62 schematic (E-C0066-4):
#   U1: the sheet's part is LM324, MAME's LM2902 -- sheet 4/4 prints LM324 (the LM2902 MAME names has its pins)
# line 403: R_AY45L_A is not a part -- the AY-3-8910's own output resistance, which MAME draws as a 1K resistor
# line 404: R_AY45L_B is not a part -- the AY-3-8910's own output resistance, which MAME draws as a 1K resistor
# line 405: R_AY45L_C is not a part -- the AY-3-8910's own output resistance, which MAME draws as a 1K resistor
# line 407: R_AY45M_A is not a part -- the AY-3-8910's own output resistance, which MAME draws as a 1K resistor
# line 408: R_AY45M_B is not a part -- the AY-3-8910's own output resistance, which MAME draws as a 1K resistor
# line 409: R_AY45M_C is not a part -- the AY-3-8910's own output resistance, which MAME draws as a 1K resistor
# line 430: SINH_DUMMY is not a part -- a 10M resistor where D6 would be: MAME's file says D6 is missing on the M62 boards in PCB pictures, though its pads exist
# Stand-ins that are not parts (the nets they drive are ports): TTL_INPUT(SINH), LOGIC_INPUT(I_SD0), LOGIC_INPUT(I_BD0), LOGIC_INPUT(I_CH0), LOGIC_INPUT(I_OH0)
#
# Compared with sheet 4/4 of the M62-A-B schematic (E-C0066-4, the conversion kit manual's
# p. 22), with sheets 2/4 and 3/4 for J4 and D6, on 2026-10-20: 80 of the file's 84 nets
# agree with the sheet, and every value printed. Three values are not fully legible: R98
# reads '6?0K' (680K is the only standard value it fits), C36's last digit is under a line,
# and R76 has none printed. The sheet prints LM324 at XU1 (written so above) and MC14584 at
# XU3.
#
# The disagreements are MAME's FIX_SCHEMATIC_ERRORS, kept as MAME has them: the sheet draws
# XU1 section B with no feedback (pin 7 drives only R45), and joins D4's and D5's cathodes,
# TR4's collector, C42 and the C43/R46/C35 node in one net -- MAME's uncorrected line --
# where MAME follows the Kung-Fu Master schematic's manual corrections, which the site does
# not hold. The MSM5205s reach the OKI node on sheet 3/4 through two resistors (one legibly
# R64) and a capacitor (apparently C46); MAME's R200, R201 and C200 are its own names for
# them, and on sheet 4/4 the line comes straight to R76, with no C200. D6 is drawn on sheet
# 2/4, between 1H pin 2 (a 74LS175) and the S-INH line, cathode at the latch; MAME's
# reason for leaving it out is board photos, which the sheet cannot settle. J4 is a
# three-terminal jumper on sheet 3/4 with no link drawn; J4 = 1 is MAME's choice.

# part U1 LM324  (MAME: XU1)
# part U2 LM358  (MAME: XU2)
# part U3 MC14584B  (MAME: XU3)

